---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * ⚠️ This file used to describe Google Analytics 4, Cloudflare Web Analytics
 * and AdSense, plus an EEA/UK consent choice. **None of them existed.** It also
 * omitted the one thing the site genuinely stores — the screen calibration.
 *
 * The rule for this page: it describes what the site does TODAY, in the
 * present tense, and nothing else. On a site whose entire pitch is that its
 * numbers are verified rather than copied, a policy listing cookies that are
 * never set is the same failure in a different file. If analytics or ads are
 * added, this page changes in the same commit — not after.
 *
 * ⚠️ That rule was then broken in the other direction. GA4 shipped in 99d303f
 * (20 Sep 2026) and this page was left saying "no analytics", "no Google
 * Analytics" and "no cookies are set" for a day — three false sentences on the
 * one page a reader consults precisely because they do not want to take the
 * rest of the site on trust. Both failures have the same shape: the page
 * described an intention rather than the deployment. Every fact below was read
 * off the live site (cookie names and the 400-day expiry from `cookieStore`,
 * the tag from the rendered `<head>`), not inferred from this repo.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, EMAIL, NOINDEX_SITE, GA_MEASUREMENT_ID } from '../config';
import { PPM_STORE_KEY } from '../data/calibration';

/* Dated from the commit that made it true, not auto-generated: a policy whose
   date moves on every build tells the reader nothing about when it changed. */
const LAST_UPDATED = '21 September 2026';

/* Read from the live cookies rather than from Google's documentation, which
   describes defaults this property may not be using. Both GA4 cookies came
   back at 400 days — the ceiling Chrome caps any cookie at. */
const GA_COOKIE_DAYS = 400;
---
<Layout
  title={`Privacy Policy — ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description={`What ${SITE_NAME} collects: Google Analytics page views and two analytics cookies. What it does not: your measurements, which never leave your browser. No account, no ads.`}
  noindex={NOINDEX_SITE}
>
  <Prose title="Privacy Policy">
    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> {LAST_UPDATED}</p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      There is no account, no sign-up and no advertising on this site today. The ring sizer itself
      runs entirely in your browser: the measurements you type, and the size it works out, are never
      sent anywhere — there is no server here to send them to.
    </p>
    <p>
      What we do collect is ordinary web analytics. Since 20 September 2026 this site has run Google
      Analytics 4, which records that a page was visited and sets two cookies to do it. That is the
      whole of it, and it is described in full below.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
    <p>Two small values, both in your browser&rsquo;s local storage, neither of which leaves it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your theme preference</strong> — light, dark or system, so the page does not flash
        the wrong colour on your next visit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your screen calibration</strong> (<code>{PPM_STORE_KEY}</code>) — a single number,
        how many pixels your display puts in a millimetre, so you do not have to hold a bank card
        against the screen every time.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Clearing this site&rsquo;s data in your browser removes both.</p>

    <h2>Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      This site runs <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong> (measurement ID <code>{GA_MEASUREMENT_ID}</code>).
      It is the only measurement script here — there is no Cloudflare Web Analytics and no other
      tracker.
    </p>
    <p>It records the ordinary things an analytics tool records:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>which page you opened, when, and which page or search sent you here;</li>
      <li>an approximate location — country, region, roughly the city — worked out from your IP address;</li>
      <li>your device type, browser, operating system and screen size;</li>
      <li>
        engagement on the page: how far you scrolled, outbound link clicks and file downloads.
        Google calls this &ldquo;enhanced measurement&rdquo; and it is switched on.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It does <strong>not</strong> receive anything you type into the ring sizer. The measurement,
      the calibration and the resulting size stay in your browser; no event carries them.
    </p>
    <p>Two cookies make this work, both first-party and both expiring after {GA_COOKIE_DAYS} days:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>_ga</code> — distinguishes one browser from another.</li>
      <li><code>_ga_{GA_MEASUREMENT_ID.replace('G-', '')}</code> — holds the session state for this property.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Being straight about the consequence: these are set when the page loads, before anyone is
      asked. There is no consent banner on this site yet. If you would rather not be counted, block
      cookies for this domain, use Google&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
        rel="nofollow noopener">opt-out add-on</a>, or any content blocker — the ring sizer works
      exactly the same either way, because it never needed the network in the first place.
    </p>

    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>None.</strong> There are no ads and no advertising cookies today. This site is
      intended to carry advertising eventually; when it does, this section will name the network,
      and visitors in the EEA and UK will be asked for consent before any advertising cookie is
      set — not afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is served by Cloudflare Pages. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes the technical
      details of each request — IP address, browser, and which page was asked for — to deliver the
      page and protect against attacks. That happens at the network level, we do not read it, and
      it is the only third party involved in serving this site. Their handling is covered by <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="nofollow noopener">Cloudflare&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Your choices</h2>
    <p>
      Clear this site&rsquo;s data in your browser to remove the theme and calibration values, and
      the two analytics cookies with them. To stay out of the analytics altogether, see the opt-out
      options above.
    </p>
    <p>
      What we hold is the aggregate Google Analytics reports — visit counts by page, country, device
      and referrer. There is no account, no email and no name attached to any of it, so we cannot
      look you up, and there is nothing personal for us to hand over or correct. If you want a
      visit deleted from those reports, write to us with the approximate date and country and we
      will submit a deletion request to Google; be aware that aggregate counts cannot be unpicked
      per visitor, which is the honest answer rather than a promise we cannot keep.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about any of this: <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a></p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
